\title{Concurrence, Exterior Algebra, and the Obstruction to\\Integer-Valued Linking Models of Two-Qubit Entanglement}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A tempting geometric proposal identifies the concurrence of a pure two-qubit state with the absolute value of a linking number derived from Hopf geometry. We show that this identification cannot hold when linking number has its ordinary integer-valued meaning. For a two-qubit amplitude vector $\psi=(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta)\in\mathbb C^4$, define the squared norm $N(\psi)=|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2+|\gamma|^2+|\delta|^2$, determinant coordinate $D(\psi)=\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma$, and concurrence $C(\psi)=2|D(\psi)|$. We prove the sharp inequality $0\le C(\psi)\le N(\psi)$, hence $C(\psi)\in[0,1]$ for normalized states. We establish that concurrence vanishes exactly when the coefficient matrix has rank one, prove global-phase invariance, and calculate unit concurrence for all four Bell states. The normalized state $(1/2,1/\sqrt2,0,1/2)$ has concurrence $1/2$, so no integer-valued invariant, including ordinary linking number, can agree in absolute value with concurrence on every normalized pure state. The viable geometric bridge is exterior algebra: concurrence is twice the norm of a determinant, or Plücker, coordinate.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A pure state of two qubits has the form
\begin{equation}
 |\psi\rangle=\alpha|00\rangle+\beta|01\rangle+\gamma|10\rangle+\delta|11\rangle.
\end{equation}
Its concurrence is $C(\psi)=2|\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma|$. The nonlocal character of entanglement naturally suggests topological imagery: two components can remain linked even while spatially separated. This motivates a strong identification in which concurrence equals the absolute value of an ordinary linking number associated with a Hopf construction.

The proposal fails for a basic reason. Concurrence varies continuously over the real interval $[0,1]$, whereas ordinary linking number is integer-valued. An explicit normalized state has concurrence $1/2$, giving a pointwise contradiction independent of any continuity assumptions or details of curve construction.

A dimensional issue also matters. Normalized two-qubit vectors form $S^7$, and the quaternionic Hopf fibration $S^7\to S^4$ has $S^3$ fibres. It does not have the circle fibres of the classical Hopf map $S^3\to S^2$. The familiar linked-circle picture therefore cannot be transferred literally.

The valid geometric core is algebraic. The four amplitudes form a $2\times2$ coefficient matrix. Its determinant is the coordinate of the exterior product of its rows. It vanishes exactly on rank-one matrices representing product states, and its norm measures departure from this rank-one locus.

\section{Definitions and the separability criterion}
\begin{definition}
A \emph{pure two-qubit amplitude vector} is $\psi=(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta)\in\mathbb C^4$. Its squared norm, coefficient matrix, determinant coordinate, and concurrence are
\begin{align}
 N(\psi)&=|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2+|\gamma|^2+|\delta|^2,\\
 A_\psi&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\\gamma&\delta\end{pmatrix},\\
 D(\psi)&=\det A_\psi=\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma,\\
 C(\psi)&=2|D(\psi)|.
\end{align}
The state is \emph{normalized} if $N(\psi)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A nonzero pure state is a \emph{product state} if there are one-qubit vectors $u=(u_0,u_1)$ and $v=(v_0,v_1)$ for which
\[
|\psi\rangle=(u_0|0\rangle+u_1|1\rangle)\otimes(v_0|0\rangle+v_1|1\rangle).
\]
Equivalently, $A_\psi$ is the outer product $(u_0,u_1)^{\mathsf T}(v_0,v_1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Determinant criterion for zero concurrence]
For every pure two-qubit amplitude vector $\psi$,
\[
 C(\psi)=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\alpha\delta=\beta\gamma.
\]
For every nonzero state, these conditions are equivalent to $A_\psi$ having rank one and to $\psi$ being a product state.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $C(\psi)=2|\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma|$, concurrence vanishes exactly when the determinant vanishes. A nonzero $2\times2$ matrix has zero determinant exactly when it has rank one. Rank-one matrices are precisely outer products of a column and a row, which are exactly the coefficient matrices obtained by expanding a tensor product of two one-qubit vectors.
\end{proof}

After projectivization, the quadratic equation $\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma=0$ describes the Segre variety $\mathbb{CP}^1\times\mathbb{CP}^1\subset\mathbb{CP}^3$. Thus separable states form a distinguished algebraic variety.

\section{The sharp concurrence bound}
The upper bound follows from the triangle inequality and Young's elementary inequality $2xy\le x^2+y^2$.

\begin{lemma}[Four-variable product bound]
For nonnegative $a,b,c,d\in\mathbb R$,
\[
 2(ad+bc)\le a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The inequalities $2ad\le a^2+d^2$ and $2bc\le b^2+c^2$ follow from $(a-d)^2\ge0$ and $(b-c)^2\ge0$. Add them.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Concurrence is bounded by squared norm]
For every $\psi\in\mathbb C^4$,
\[
 C(\psi)\le N(\psi).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The triangle inequality and multiplicativity of the complex norm give
\[
 |\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma|
 \le |\alpha||\delta|+|\beta||\gamma|.
\]
Multiplying by two and applying the preceding lemma to $(|\alpha|,|\beta|,|\gamma|,|\delta|)$ yields
\[
 C(\psi)\le |\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2+|\gamma|^2+|\delta|^2=N(\psi).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unit-interval theorem]
Every normalized pure two-qubit state satisfies $0\le C(\psi)\le1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity follows because concurrence is twice a norm. The upper bound follows from the theorem and $N(\psi)=1$.
\end{proof}

Both endpoints are sharp: product states realize zero and Bell states realize one. Equality in the upper-bound proof requires compatible equality in the complex triangle inequality together with $|\alpha|=|\delta|$ and $|\beta|=|\gamma|$. These conditions motivate the classification of all maximizers under local unitary transformations.

\section{Symmetry and Bell states}
\begin{theorem}[Global-phase invariance]
If $u\in\mathbb C$ and $|u|=1$, then $C(u\psi)=C(\psi)$, where $u\psi=(u\alpha,u\beta,u\gamma,u\delta)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The determinant is homogeneous of degree two:
\[
D(u\psi)=u^2D(\psi).
\]
Consequently $C(u\psi)=2|u|^2|D(\psi)|=C(\psi)$.
\end{proof}

For $s\in\{-1,1\}$ define the four Bell states
\[
 |\Phi_s\rangle=\frac{|00\rangle+s|11\rangle}{\sqrt2},
 \qquad
 |\Psi_s\rangle=\frac{|01\rangle+s|10\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Bell normalization and maximality]
For both signs $s\in\{-1,1\}$,
\[
 N(\Phi_s)=N(\Psi_s)=1,
 \qquad
 C(\Phi_s)=C(\Psi_s)=1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each state has exactly two nonzero amplitudes of modulus $1/\sqrt2$, so its squared norm is one. Their determinants are
\[
D(\Phi_s)=\frac{s}{2},\qquad D(\Psi_s)=-\frac{s}{2}.
\]
Thus each determinant has norm $1/2$ and each concurrence equals one. The unit-interval theorem shows this is maximal.
\end{proof}

Endpoint agreement supports a visual analogy between Bell states and maximal linking, but it does not establish equality of the two invariants throughout state space.

\section{The half-concurrence obstruction}
Consider the state
\[
 |\chi\rangle=\frac12|00\rangle+\frac1{\sqrt2}|01\rangle+\frac12|11\rangle,
 \qquad
 \chi=\left(\frac12,\frac1{\sqrt2},0,\frac12\right).
\]

\begin{lemma}[Normalized obstruction witness]
The state $\chi$ is normalized and has concurrence $1/2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Directly,
\[
 N(\chi)=\frac14+\frac12+0+\frac14=1.
\]
Moreover,
\[
 D(\chi)=\frac12\cdot\frac12-\frac1{\sqrt2}\cdot0=\frac14,
\]
so $C(\chi)=2|1/4|=1/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal integer-valued obstruction]
There is no integer-valued function $L$ on normalized pure two-qubit states such that
\[
 C(\psi)=|L(\psi)|
\]
for every normalized state $\psi$. In particular, concurrence cannot universally equal the absolute value of an ordinary linking number.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If such $L$ existed, the witness would satisfy
\[
 \frac12=C(\chi)=|L(\chi)|.
\]
The absolute value of an integer is a nonnegative integer and cannot equal $1/2$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

This proof is independent of any proposed construction of curves. It excludes every integer assignment under the universal equality claim. Continuity gives a complementary perspective: $S^7$ is connected and concurrence is continuous with intermediate values, whereas any continuous integer-valued function on a connected space is constant.

\section{Hopf geometry and exterior algebra}
Normalized vectors in $\mathbb C^4$ form $S^7$. Identifying $\mathbb C^4$ with $\mathbb H^2$ gives the quaternionic Hopf fibration
\[
 S^3\hookrightarrow S^7\longrightarrow S^4.
\]
Its fibres are three-spheres. This differs from the classical complex Hopf fibration
\[
 S^1\hookrightarrow S^3\longrightarrow S^2,
\]
whose circle fibres produce the familiar Hopf link. Describing the fibres of $S^7\to S^4$ as linked circles therefore conflates two fibrations. Higher-dimensional linking phenomena may be relevant, but an ordinary homological linking invariant remains discrete.

The exact positive bridge is exterior algebra. Let the rows of $A_\psi$ be $r_1=(\alpha,\beta)$ and $r_2=(\gamma,\delta)$. Then
\[
 r_1\wedge r_2=(\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma)e_1\wedge e_2,
 \qquad
 C(\psi)=2\|r_1\wedge r_2\|.
\]
The wedge product vanishes exactly when the rows are dependent. Concurrence is thus twice an area norm, measuring the failure of the coefficient matrix to collapse to rank one.

The determinant is the unique $2\times2$ minor in this case. For larger bipartite systems, families of minors form Plücker coordinates. Their norms naturally generalize the rank-detection role of the two-qubit determinant and suggest a hierarchy of entanglement quantities.

\section{Algorithms and numerical demonstrations}
A stable evaluator first computes $N(\psi)$. For nonzero input intended as a physical state, divide all amplitudes by $\sqrt{N(\psi)}$. Then compute $D(\psi)=\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma$ and return $2|D(\psi)|$. For a fixed two-qubit state this uses constant arithmetic work. Normalizing a general amplitude list is linear in its length.

A transparent demonstration suite contains the product state $|00\rangle$ with concurrence zero, all four Bell states with concurrence one, and $\chi$ with concurrence $1/2$. Random normalized complex Gaussian vectors illustrate the continuum of intermediate values, although random sampling is illustrative rather than a proof.

To expose the integer obstruction numerically, define
\[
 \Delta_{\mathbb Z}(c)=\min_{n\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}}|c-n|.
\]
For $c\in[0,1]$, this is $\min(c,1-c)$. It vanishes on product and Bell endpoints but equals $1/2$ for the witness. Thus endpoint-only tests are unable to distinguish concurrence from a binary linking picture, while the witness maximizes the discrepancy within the unit interval.

\section{Applications and limitations}
The determinant gives a constant-time pure-state separability diagnostic. With experimental amplitudes, uncertainty must be propagated, because a tiny nonzero determinant may reflect measurement error.

Geometrically, $D(\psi)=0$ identifies the Segre variety. This opens the study of tangent and normal directions and of sharp comparisons between concurrence and Euclidean or Fubini--Study distance from the separable locus.

The obstruction also supplies design constraints for a replacement Hopf quantity. A viable functional should be real-valued and continuous, invariant under global phase and preferably local unitaries, vanish exactly on product states, and equal one exactly on maximally entangled states. Normalized integrals, holonomies, calibrated volumes, or averaged linking kernels can vary continuously and therefore avoid the integer-codomain obstruction.

The results do not assert that topology is irrelevant to entanglement. They rule out one literal universal equality. Nor do they treat mixed-state concurrence, which requires additional convex and spectral structure. Finally, Bell-state maximality is proved here, but a complete local-unitary classification of all maximizers remains a separate task.

\section{Future directions}
A first direction is a canonically normalized real-valued functional on quaternionic Hopf geometry matching the determinant benchmark. A second is an exterior-algebra hierarchy based on minors of coefficient flattenings for multipartite states. A third is a full classification of equality in $C(\psi)\le N(\psi)$, translating the simultaneous equality conditions into unitary-invariant language.

A fourth direction seeks optimal constants comparing concurrence with distance to the Segre variety under Euclidean and Fubini--Study metrics. Finally, fixed-concurrence level sets, after quotienting by global phase, may have a common topology for values strictly between zero and one, with changes only at the product and maximally entangled strata. This is a setting in which topology can interact with concurrence without forcing a continuous quantity to become an integer.

\section{Conclusion}
For pure two-qubit states, concurrence is governed by the determinant $C(\psi)=2|\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma|$. This formula yields the exact product-state locus, the sharp normalized range $[0,1]$, global-phase invariance, and unit concurrence for every Bell state. It also produces a normalized state of concurrence $1/2$, proving that no ordinary integer-valued linking number can equal concurrence universally.

The corrected geometric interpretation is exterior-algebraic: concurrence is the norm of a wedge-product coordinate measuring failure of rank one. Hopf geometry may still yield a useful quantitative interpretation, but any universal replacement must respect the real-valued and continuous nature of entanglement.

